WEBVTT

00:00:00.101 --> 00:00:01.116
One treat now.

00:00:01.126 --> 00:00:02.272
Or two later.

00:00:02.282 --> 00:00:06.010
Psychologist Walter Mischel
left preschoolers alone with

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:07.165
a marshmallow.

00:00:07.175 --> 00:00:08.915
Hold out, and you'd get a second

00:00:08.925 --> 00:00:09.606
one.

00:00:09.616 --> 00:00:12.043
Years later, kids who waited longer were

00:00:12.053 --> 00:00:13.874
doing better as teens.

00:00:13.884 --> 00:00:16.111
So willpower at four seemed to predict

00:00:16.121 --> 00:00:17.062
the future.

00:00:17.072 --> 00:00:19.107
But the original children were a small,

00:00:19.117 --> 00:00:20.272
narrow group.

00:00:20.282 --> 00:00:23.822
In 2018, researchers used data on over

00:00:23.832 --> 00:00:27.222
nine hundred US children,
focusing on those

00:00:27.232 --> 00:00:29.692
whose mothers hadn't finished college.

00:00:29.702 --> 00:00:31.330
Waiters still did a bit better in

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.102
school at fifteen.

00:00:33.115 --> 00:00:35.316
But the link was half as strong.

00:00:35.326 --> 00:00:37.955
Then the researchers adjusted the numbers.

00:00:37.965 --> 00:00:40.405
The link shrank by two thirds once

00:00:40.415 --> 00:00:43.295
family background and
home life were counted.

00:00:43.305 --> 00:00:46.333
Waiting for a marshmallow, it turns out,

00:00:46.343 --> 00:00:48.145
is partly tangled up with the life

00:00:48.155 --> 00:00:49.280
around you.
